le bit more  confidence in that side of the airport  but those talks have not concluded i  just checked this morning with sources  and it's really uh uncertain yet whether  that that agreement can be concluded  with turkey  now terry the security situation in  afghanistan is of course  uh unstable at the moment there are  fears of a civil war with the taliban  on the rise and warlords battling for  power  after being nearly 20 years in  afghanistan do nato troops  not have a certain responsibility to  stay and help  that's certainly an argument that has  been made and one that  that the u.s and uh and nato considered  uh it  once they made the decision basically  they came to the conclusion that  that this war was not one that they  could win that the taliban could not be  uh finally vanquished militarily uh  they really did rush to the exit um and  this uh moving up of the deadline of of  u.s troops to leave is something that we  had heard rumored  moving it up from september 11th which  was the official deadline  um but i i would not be surprised that  within the next couple of days  um by the fourth of july we hear that uh  all u.s troops uh are out of the country  that is quite a speed up terry tell us  what nato achieved in its 20 years in  afghanistan  the end chapter is not written yet but  signs do not look  good even u.s and nato  commanders confirmed that the taliban is  retaking territory even before the  pull-out was announced the taliban  controlled  half the country i mean i think that's a  statistic that would shock a lot of  people after 20 years of  u.s and nato forces there people on the  ground are very scared  uh it without without the u.s and nato  forces there  a lot of the security that had been  fragile but at least uh  somewhat intact will be gone and i think  nato would have a very hard time calling  this a success story  and we've seen the us dial back what  what it expected to achieve there to  saying  well we got osama bin laden and that